{"name":"Egg Yolk","hex":"#ffce81","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"egg yolk","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The central part of a chicken’s (or other bird’s) egg, yellow when fresh."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/egg_yolk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Yolk","blocks":[{"text":"Among animals which produce eggs, the yolk is the nutrient-bearing portion of the egg, the primary function of which is to supply food for the development of the embryo. Some types of egg contain no yolk, for example because they are laid in situations where the food supply is sufficient or because the embryo develops in the parent's body, which supplies the food, usually through a placenta. Reproductive systems in which the mother's body supplies the embryo directly are said to be matrotrophic; those in which the embryo is supplied by yolk are said to be lecithotrophic. In many species, such as all birds, and most reptiles and insects, the yolk takes the form of a special storage organ constructed in the reproductive tract of the mother. In many other animals, especially very small species such as some fish and invertebrates, the yolk material is not in a special organ, but inside the egg cell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Raw_egg.jpg/330px-Raw_egg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yolk"},{"title":"Egg","blocks":[{"text":"An egg is an organic vessel grown by an animal to carry a possibly fertilized egg cell – a zygote. Within the vessel, an embryo is incubated until it has developed enough to survive on its own, at which point the animal hatches. Reproductive structures similar to the egg in other kingdoms are termed \"spores\", or in spermatophytes \"seeds\", or in gametophytes \"egg cells\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg/330px-Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egg"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Double_and_single_yolk_hen_eggs.png/500px-Double_and_single_yolk_hen_eggs.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Double_and_single_yolk_hen_eggs.png/500px-Double_and_single_yolk_hen_eggs.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Three similarly sized eggs in a hot frying pan.  Each of the two yolks in the double-yolked eggs are smaller than typical for that size of egg.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADouble_and_single_yolk_hen_eggs.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Egg_and_maxi_egg_1.jpg/250px-Egg_and_maxi_egg_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Egg_and_maxi_egg_1.jpg/500px-Egg_and_maxi_egg_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Egg_and_maxi_egg_1.jpg/250px-Egg_and_maxi_egg_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEgg_and_maxi_egg_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Egg_and_maxi_egg_2.jpg/250px-Egg_and_maxi_egg_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Egg_and_maxi_egg_2.jpg/500px-Egg_and_maxi_egg_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Egg_and_maxi_egg_2.jpg/250px-Egg_and_maxi_egg_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","